WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.351 --> 00:00:03.934
(upbeat music)

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.610
- Okay, welcome.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.340
I'm Emily Conover,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.400
Senior Physics Writer for Science News.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.810
In honor of the hundredth
anniversary of our publication,

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.000
we are interviewing
some leaders in science

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.560
on some big questions.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.130
I am delighted to be tackling
the topic of black holes

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.020
with Dr. Andrea Ghez

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.390
an astrophysicist at UCLA

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.390
and notably a winner of the
2020 Nobel Prize in Physics

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:44.170
for her work that unveiled
the supermassive black hole

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.990
at the center of our home
galaxy, the Milky Way.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.600
Thanks so much for
speaking with me, Dr. Ghez.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.970
- Oh, it's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.710
- So I think it's fair to say

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.368
that black holes are
having a moment right now.

00:00:59.368 --> 00:01:02.810
On top of your own research
and the Nobel Prize,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.830
we have the gravitational
wave observatories,

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.110
which in 2015,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.810
first spotted two black holes

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.730
spiraling together and merging.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.190
We have the first image
of a black hole silhouette

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.690
from the Event Horizon Telescope in 2019,

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.640
and lots of other, too many
exciting results to mention.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.050
So I think it's really fitting

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.233
that we're talking about this
topic today and right now.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.380
And I thought maybe to start,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.350
for our viewers who might be
less familiar with black holes

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.440
or might only know them
from science fiction movies,

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.380
can you start by just
defining what a black hole is?

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.213
- Sure.

00:01:38.213 --> 00:01:41.710
Black holes are remarkably
simple given the fact

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.330
that they really push our
understanding of physics forward

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.160
or challenge our understanding of physics.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.900
So a black hole is very
simply a region of space

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.050
where the pull of gravity is so intense

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.563
that nothing can escape
it, not even light.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.760
You can also think of it just as an object

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.520
whose mass is confined to zero volume.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.930
And so the idea of density,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.850
which is mass divided by volume,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.870
that goes to infinity,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:10.703
And that's like the big,

00:02:10.703 --> 00:02:12.100
giant red arrow that says you don't have

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.863
your description of
physics correct right here.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:16.793
- Yeah.

00:02:16.793 --> 00:02:17.626
And that's sort of where

00:02:17.626 --> 00:02:20.993
all the weird puzzles come in, right?

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.230
And your work in particular,

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.450
you focus on supermassive black holes,

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.720
one super massive black
hole in particular.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.420
Can you talk a little bit
about the distinction between

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.920
these sort of super massive giant guys

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.593
and the smaller black holes?

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:36.753
- Sure. I mean,

00:02:36.753 --> 00:02:39.800
in astrophysics we think of
two classes of black holes.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.700
And in fact,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.960
these two classes nicely demonstrate

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.070
the two ways in which
science is often done.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.020
So the first ones that were
recognized or understood

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.760
are what we call the
stellar-mass black holes.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.090
And they're the outcome of how stars

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.690
that start off their life
as very massive stars,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.420
with more than 30 times
the mass of the sun,

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.690
are expected to end up,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.010
so predicted by theory

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.480
and demonstrated
beautifully observationally

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.693
with the gravitational wave detectors.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.280
The supermassive black
holes are much more massive,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.690
as the name suggests.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:15.910
So rather than being

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.580
on order of 10 times the mass of the sun,

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.413
they're more like

00:03:19.413 --> 00:03:22.430
a million to a billion
times the mass of the sun.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:27.430
And we think that they reside
at the center of galaxies,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.430
so that each galaxy is thought to form

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.120
with one central supermassive black hole.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.940
So these were found observationally,

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.140
and now we're on the hunt

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.753
to understand theoretically
how they formed.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:44.130
- One thing you know, about
both types of black holes,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.430
there's sort of this nexus

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.610
of lots of these really big questions

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.130
in physics and astronomy.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.440
And they cover sort of
a variety of different

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.930
sort of sub-fields, right?

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.600
So you have a fundamental
tests of general relativity,

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.623
which is Einstein's theory of gravity.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.640
Questions about how galaxies form,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.330
you know, everything in between, right?

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.150
So I'm curious what you think,

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.590
what is the reason that
black holes in general

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.560
are such fruitful grounds for research

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.763
on such a variety of topics?

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.630
- Oh, that's an interesting question.

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:20.270
I mean, in the case of
fundamental physics,

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:23.127
which really crosses both
the stellar-mass black holes

00:04:23.127 --> 00:04:25.193
and the supermassive black holes,

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.390
you really have very fundamental questions

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.180
about how our universe works.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.930
We only have four known
forces and gravity is one.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.460
It's the one we're most familiar with

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.610
in terms of our intuition here on earth.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.480
You throw a ball up, it comes back down.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.103
And yet it's actually the least
tested of the four forces.

00:04:45.024 --> 00:04:48.900
So black holes provide this avenue

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.430
for furthering our understanding
of fundamental physics.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.120
And in particular,

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.230
our inability at the moment

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.740
to bring together the two
fields of quantum mechanics,

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.370
which is the study of
things that are very small,

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.810
together with the field
of general relativity,

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.433
which is our best description
of how gravity works.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.540
In the case of supermassive black holes,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.930
these things reside at
the center of galaxies

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.930
where you have extreme environments

00:05:16.961 --> 00:05:19.300
in many, many different ways.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.350
I mean, not only is the
gravitational field extreme

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.050
because of the black hole itself,

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.760
but the centers of galaxies are extreme

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.580
in many other aspects,

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.230
such as the stellar density.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.160
So people often refer to
the center of galaxies

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:34.390
as like the urban hub.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:35.223
It's like, you know,

00:05:35.223 --> 00:05:37.310
the center of New York City
and we're out in the half,

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:38.740
you know, we're halfway out in the galaxy.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.723
So we're out in the calm suburbs.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.550
But almost any way you
can describe a galaxy

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.470
becomes more extreme.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.380
The magnetic fields, the winds,

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.550
just the activity in
general is really extreme.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:55.000
So it means that you have
an opportunity to understand

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.710
the physical processes that are associated

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.990
with multiple aspects of a galaxy

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.070
in the extreme environment
of the center of galaxies.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.260
So, in the case of the
supermassive black holes,

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.520
you get that extra layer
of trying to understand

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.250
not only the formation and
evolution of these galaxies,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.970
which we think the black
hole plays a central role in,

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.415
but just physics,

00:06:18.415 --> 00:06:21.320
sort of physics of the extremes.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:22.580
- Yeah, that's great.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.430
I know physicists always love

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.470
to look at the most extreme example

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.090
and use that to test their understanding.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.530
So I think black holes

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.430
are definitely a good example of that.

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:35.720
Great.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:37.960
So I'm curious if you could tell us

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.260
your sort of scientific origin story.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.200
So what was it that originally
drew you into this topic?

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.159
Or how did you get started

00:06:45.159 --> 00:06:47.633
with your fascination with black holes?

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.593
- Origin stories are always complicated,

00:06:50.593 --> 00:06:52.083
astrophysically or otherwise.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.325
So mine,

00:06:54.325 --> 00:06:57.140
I can tell you a narrative
that will make sense,

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.120
but it wasn't actually as
clean as the narrative,

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:00.420
which I'll also share.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.440
So the family lore is that
when I was four years old

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.660
and I do remember this,

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.200
the first moon landing was happening.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:12.210
So I got excited about
thinking about the universe,

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:15.570
certainly started to stay
up late at night, wondering,

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.580
worrying about, you know,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.653
the enormity, the
timescales, the boundary.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.660
And the family legend is

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.870
I announced that I wanted to
be the first woman on the moon.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.800
I mean today I joke, you
know, the job's still open.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.660
But I certainly got more interested

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.083
in pursuing astrophysics from the ground.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:36.443
But at the same time,

00:07:36.443 --> 00:07:38.360
I also wanted to be a ballerina.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:39.650
As little kids want to do,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.090
they have multiple ideas about,

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:44.392
or ways of thinking about their future.

00:07:44.392 --> 00:07:47.620
So while I got exposed to it early on

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.120
and was fascinated by it,

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.343
I wasn't singular or
certain in my path forward.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.440
But what I do remember,

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:00.120
and I think really connects,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.470
is that I've always been
interested in solving problems.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:07.000
So ultimately my interest in
dance got into choreography,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.630
which is basically a
problem-solving exercise

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:10.880
of how do you put this, you know,

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.373
this dance story together.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.800
And I became very good at math.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.040
So I actually went off to college

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.513
wanting to be a math major,

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.210
and soon discovered

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.493
it was much more esoteric than I thought.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.170
And at that point I was quite
interested in black holes,

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:28.971
so I switched to physics

00:08:28.971 --> 00:08:33.160
as maybe more an experimental approach

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:36.273
as opposed to a theoretical
approach to black holes.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:39.480
- Great.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.803
So when you were starting your work,

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.010
can you describe for us

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.770
what was sort of the scientific attitude

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.420
towards black holes?

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.860
You know, were people
confident that they existed

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.170
or was there still some question,

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.763
and then how did your work
sort of move that forward?

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:01.960
- Yeah. So when I started this project

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.550
there was definitely
the idea or the notion

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.170
that supermassive black holes

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.000
might exist at the center of galaxies.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.370
And in particular,

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:16.120
the state of the field was that, you know,

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:19.500
there's a small class or
about 10% of all galaxies

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.470
have an unusual amount of energy

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:23.800
coming out from their centers.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.580
They're called active galactic nuclei.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:26.890
I mean, their name really says it all.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.229
Their nuclei, or their
centers, are very active.

00:09:29.229 --> 00:09:31.310
And it was hypothesized

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.143
that what was driving all this activity

00:09:32.143 --> 00:09:36.450
was a super massive
black hole at the center,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.190
so that would be the central engine.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:42.640
And roughly at that same time,

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.419
people were asking

00:09:44.419 --> 00:09:47.570
could it possibly be
true that all galaxies

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.200
harbor supermassive black holes?

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.830
And in the case of normal galaxies,

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.310
these are black holes that
are basically on a diet,

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:57.680
are lacking material falling
on to the black hole,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.820
which is how we thought

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.940
the active galactic
nuclei were being powered.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:03.030
But in our own case,

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.010
in the case of our own Milky Way,

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.096
the idea,

00:10:06.096 --> 00:10:09.310
you know, our galaxy is an
ordinary, garden-variety galaxy,

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.033
nothing special about us.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:13.900
The idea there is that

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.450
there was very little gas at the center.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.900
And so perhaps this is a black
hole that was simply starved.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.540
So that really offered,

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.300
I mean, that's what
also introduced the idea

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.210
that the Milky Way is a
great place to ask this

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.770
because it's the closest example

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.610
of a supermassive black hole
that we'll ever have to study.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:36.610
The next closest one is a
hundred times further away.

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.150
Now, while there was that idea,

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:42.410
there were also some
counter-arguments to the idea

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.551
that our galaxy could harbor
a supermassive black hole,

00:10:45.551 --> 00:10:49.680
or just that all galaxies
had supermassive black holes.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.940
And that was that there
were very young stars

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.630
seen in the proximity,

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:58.440
not as close as we ended up looking,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.210
but out at further distances.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.690
So already, the existence
of these young stars

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:06.130
gave some trouble to the notion

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:07.580
that there was a black hole there,

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:11.060
because black holes produce
these very strong tidal forces.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:14.950
So the idea that gravity
depends on distance,

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.520
so that the pull on one side of an object

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.580
is greater than the pull on
the other side of the object,

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.700
so much so that it just distorts

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.860
or pulls apart the object itself.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:24.693
And stars,

00:11:24.693 --> 00:11:28.130
baby stars start off
as very fragile clouds

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:29.720
of gas and dust.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.147
So there was this problem
already that people said,

00:11:33.147 --> 00:11:34.940
"Well, look, there's not enough gas

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.750
or dense enough gas to
overcome this problem."

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.180
So that's an argument
against the black hole.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.180
So that's really where the argument was

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.800
in the early nineties, when we jumped in.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.380
You know, a nice scenario
for why they should be there

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.133
and an argument against them.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:54.250
So that was really the state of the field.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:55.700
Although I should mention one other thing,

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.050
which is that we did know

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.530
that there was an unusual
radio source at the middle,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.057
it's name is Sagittarius A*.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.030
So Sagittarius

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.910
because it's in the
constellation of Sagittarius.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.080
So if you look up at night

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.090
and you look for the
constellation of the tea cup,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.010
it pours into the center of the galaxy.

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.050
So that's how you can find
the center of the galaxy

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:17.333
if you're out camping.

00:12:19.711 --> 00:12:22.820
And A, because it was the
brightest radio source

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.935
and star, because people were
trying to be very clever.

00:12:25.935 --> 00:12:30.140
They were borrowing notation
from nuclear physics

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:32.800
where the asterisk meant excited state.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:36.110
So they were seeing gas that
didn't look like a star.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.310
So it was Sag-A, really not a star.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.173
So it's one of those names that's,

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:48.210
so we really should say
Sag-A, not a star. (laughs)

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.010
So they thought it was like a low,

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:53.370
very, very, very, very,
very, very faint equivalent

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:55.833
of the active galactic nuclei centers.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:57.773
- Right.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:03.490
So then you come in and
start looking at these stars

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.390
that are very close to the, you know,

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:07.780
Sag-A star, which you know,

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.790
at that point in time was not known

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.680
whether it was a supermassive
black hole or not.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:15.040
How were you able to reveal

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.253
that in fact that is what
we were seeing there.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.910
- So the idea of the work that we did

00:13:23.921 --> 00:13:26.320
was that stars that are very close

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.460
should provide the most direct evidence

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:32.530
for a supermassive black
hole through their motions.

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.810
So if you can discover
stars that are there

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.020
and track their motion,

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.280
so basically watch their orbits,

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.631
these stars should orbit
the center of the galaxy

00:13:41.631 --> 00:13:44.210
for the same reason that
planets orbit the sun.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:45.650
So it's just gravity.

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:50.120
And each orbit tells you how
much mass is inside its orbit.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:53.700
And then the size of
the scale of that orbit

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.770
tells you the region that
you can find the mass too.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.230
So the proof of the black
hole is lots of mass

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:03.090
inside a very small volume or radius.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:03.923
And in fact,

00:14:03.923 --> 00:14:05.560
you'd like to get inside
the Schwarzschild radius,

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:07.620
'cause that's the point
at which gravity overcomes

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.310
all other known forces
and forces that mass

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.910
to become the infinitesimally small object

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.343
that we know of as a black hole.

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.210
So, you want to see stars

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:19.540
as close to the center of the galaxy.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:20.720
So the first challenge was,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.250
can we find them, can we discover them?

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:26.840
And since you want to get close,

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:27.960
you need to go to wavelengths

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.160
where you can see the starlight.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.600
So radio wavelengths,

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:34.040
you don't see much light from the stars,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.090
it's mostly dominated by the gas.

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.263
So we went to the infrared.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.830
You can go to the optical or
the infrared to see starlight,

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:44.246
but at the center of the galaxy,

00:14:44.246 --> 00:14:46.750
there's lots of dust between us

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:48.770
down the center of the galaxy.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:53.130
And that dust is very good
at absorbing optical light,

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:54.770
what our eye detects.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.060
So it's basically invisible
at optical wavelengths,

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.950
but if you go to the infrared,

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:00.780
you can see that starlight.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.880
So that's why all our work
has been done in the infrared.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:06.020
Next, you want to go to a big telescope.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:07.320
The larger your telescope,

00:15:09.922 --> 00:15:11.600
the better the angular resolution.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.000
So you want to see stars
as close as possible,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:14.833
so it's really important

00:15:14.833 --> 00:15:16.823
that you get to high angular resolution.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.940
So the larger the telescope in principle,

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.340
the smaller the scale
that you can actually see.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:25.610
And the largest telescope in the world

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:29.160
that can see infrared light
right now is the Keck telescope.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:30.250
This is out in Hawaii,

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:31.540
but it's on the ground,

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:33.200
and so you have to contend
with the atmosphere.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.690
So the next challenge is you
have to beat the atmosphere.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.760
And so that's what we we've
done a lot of work on,

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:39.780
is trying to figure out
how to beat the atmosphere

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:42.380
so that you can actually see these stars

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:43.893
and track their motions.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:50.180
- Yeah, I have to say that
the videos that you've made

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.550
of showing this data over
decades of these stars

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:56.630
moving around the supermassive black hole,

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:59.350
they are so fascinating to watch.

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.853
I just love kind of marveling at the fact

00:16:01.853 --> 00:16:03.900
that we can even see these stars

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.890
and measure them moving around this,

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:09.340
you know, extremely massive black hole.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:10.670
It blows me away every time.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.640
So I'm just curious,

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.660
when were you first able to see that

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:16.670
and, you know,

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.180
what was the feeling that you had

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:19.980
when you were first able to do that?

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:21.870
- Oh gosh.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:22.900
So this actually goes back to

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:25.740
when I first joined the faculty at UCLA,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.280
that was in 1994.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.330
So I joined the faculty

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.230
because I really wanted
to do this experiment.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.410
I wanted access to the Keck telescope.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.580
Keck is co-owned by the
University of California

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:36.413
and Caltech,

00:16:36.413 --> 00:16:38.320
so this to me was my dream job.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.467
So I put my very first
proposal in, in 1994,

00:16:41.467 --> 00:16:43.750
and it was turned down. (laughs)

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:44.810
So it was turned down

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:48.790
because people hadn't done this
diffraction-limited imaging

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:49.623
at Keck yet.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:53.013
So you know,

00:16:53.013 --> 00:16:54.640
there was a lot of skepticism
about whether or not Keck,

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:56.360
which is a segmented telescope,

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.720
could actually achieve
diffraction-limited resolution,

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.860
in spite of the fact that
it was designed to do so.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:05.100
So it took us a year to convince people

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:06.673
that our approach would work.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.003
So we started in 1995 as a result.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:14.370
So the first thing we needed to do

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:17.330
was demonstrate that the
technique would work.

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:19.750
And in '90, I guess we
actually did that in '94.

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:23.000
We just had to prove that
the technique would work.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.680
And then in '95, we
actually saw the images.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.060
So you could prove that they could work,

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:30.600
this business works at
the center of the galaxy.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.533
So that was really exciting in '95.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.570
But you haven't seen the motion yet.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:37.560
So the original approach

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.070
was just to see how things
move on the plane of the sky.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:41.790
So you need two images.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.513
So we did that in '96.

00:17:43.513 --> 00:17:44.346
You have two points,

00:17:44.346 --> 00:17:46.120
two points determine a line.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:46.990
And, oh my goodness,

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:50.110
I cannot tell you how
exciting it was in 1996

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:51.890
to get that second image.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.190
And of course, you know,

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:56.200
there are lots of
challenges along the way,

00:17:56.200 --> 00:18:00.010
including the first night
in '96 was weathered out.

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:02.960
The second night, I think
we had instrument problems.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.090
And it was the third time

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:07.470
we went in and actually got the data.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.050
So there's nothing like
a little bit of obstacle

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.663
to make the success even sweeter.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:17.440
We didn't publish until '97

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.100
because we actually wanted three points

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:23.080
to make sure we understood
how well we were doing,

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:24.660
because of course there
are lots of challenges

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.770
associated with making these measurements.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:28.210
So it was '97

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.533
that we submitted our
first paper on doing this.

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:34.503
I'll say, actually,

00:18:34.503 --> 00:18:35.750
I can say a little bit
more about that phase,

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:36.670
which was also interesting

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:38.530
but really fun because we only thought

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.930
we were going to do a
three-year experiment.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:41.840
- Wow.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.700
- So the original idea was
just to get the velocities,

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.703
'cause you can prove statistically,

00:18:48.547 --> 00:18:51.150
you can use ensemble averages

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:53.920
or velocity dispersions rather,

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:56.780
to get the enclosed mass.

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:57.613
But at that point,

00:18:57.613 --> 00:18:58.670
people were pushing back.

00:18:58.670 --> 00:18:59.880
There was a lot of skepticism

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.117
that it wasn't actually a black hole.

00:19:01.117 --> 00:19:04.450
And the particle physicists
got into the act at this point

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:05.580
and started suggesting

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.877
that there were other kinds
of objects it could be.

00:19:08.877 --> 00:19:12.390
And in particular, the idea
that it could be a fermion ball.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:16.160
So these are objects that are,

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:17.860
I guess they're like neutron stars.

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:20.800
So rather than being a
pure object of neutrons,

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:23.810
these are more primitive particles,

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:25.560
which means that it's more compact.

00:19:26.759 --> 00:19:28.710
And there were also
suggestions that these objects,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:31.137
the fast-moving stars that we
were seeing really close in,

00:19:31.137 --> 00:19:35.231
weren't actually bound to
the center of the galaxy.

00:19:35.231 --> 00:19:36.064
It was really fun.

00:19:36.064 --> 00:19:37.610
I guess it was really fun at
this point in the experiment

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:39.890
because people were starting
to push back on the idea

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.011
that the velocities that were seeing

00:19:42.011 --> 00:19:44.450
were from the black hole.

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:47.370
And you can do a freshman physics
calculation that tells you

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.610
that you should be soon
seeing the accelerations,

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:51.730
if this is indeed a black hole.

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:54.227
So we just kept going and soon enough,

00:19:54.227 --> 00:19:56.430
things started to curve.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:20:00.620
And at that point you can do
some again, simple things.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.280
There were three stars that
showed the first accelerations

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:05.040
and each acceleration should point to

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.430
where the black hole is because
it's a central potential.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:11.020
And the central potential
should be radial.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:12.670
And even in projection,

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:14.710
you should get these
vectors that point to,

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:16.180
this is where the black hole is.

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:21.180
And so all of a sudden you
actually can show that this mass

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:23.690
is associated with that
unusual radio source,

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:28.690
Sag-A*, to a very high degree of accuracy.

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:33.000
And again, pushes you to the next level.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.060
Because at that point,

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:35.250
you know that the orbit

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:38.280
should only be on order of 10 years long.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:39.760
We'd been going for five years,

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:41.210
what's another five years?

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:44.830
And at that point, it was
clear that we should continue.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:46.700
And people, even at that point,

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:50.543
were starting to anticipate
the power of these orbits.

00:20:50.543 --> 00:20:52.820
That these orbits could show you

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:54.950
that not only that there was a black hole,

00:20:54.950 --> 00:20:56.530
but could introduce new ways

00:20:56.530 --> 00:21:00.690
of testing Einstein's theories
of general relativity.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.230
So I have to say that paper,

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:04.600
the acceleration paper,

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:07.460
which came out in 2000,

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:09.600
was really a transformative moment.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:12.460
I think that's at the point
that we really understood

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:17.460
how much science could come
from this sort of work.

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:20.912
It was really,

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:23.423
it was really an exciting moment.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:26.940
- Yeah. And you're still of course

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:30.150
working on the same area, you know,

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.050
the center of the galaxy today, right?

00:21:33.133 --> 00:21:36.440
So, I mean, I guess I'm
wondering, you know,

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.080
do you ever lose interest?

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:43.310
I mean, how does this keep
your attention for so long,

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:45.433
you know, from the '90s until now?

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:49.320
- Well, what's been so
fascinating about this

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:50.880
is there's been such evolution,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:53.470
both in terms of the data themselves,

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.860
'cause you keep going.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:22:00.070
And of course with the game
of orbits, time really helps.

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:02.310
The power of these measurements

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.760
increases dramatically with time.

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:06.060
But along the way,

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:10.770
we've also had the benefit
of advances in technology.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:14.020
So after the first decade,

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:17.010
we moved from a very simple
approach of speckle imaging,

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:19.680
which only allowed us to take
images at a single wavelength

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:20.840
and that were not very deep,

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:22.010
to adaptive optics,

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:24.050
which was a much more powerful technique

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:27.803
and opened up new kinds of measurements.

00:22:27.803 --> 00:22:31.840
And the most important was our
ability to get spectroscopy,

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:33.970
which gave us motion
along the third dimension

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.470
along our line of sight,

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:39.180
but also told us what kind
of stars we were looking at,

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.470
which revealed a host of new questions,

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:43.813
astrophysical questions.

00:22:43.813 --> 00:22:45.850
And that along with being able

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:47.970
to take images at different wavelengths

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:52.320
just opened up many new questions.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:56.030
So I think that's part of
what's been so fascinating

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:58.240
or captivating about this project,

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:03.169
is the continual opportunities
to go in new directions.

00:23:03.169 --> 00:23:05.977
is the continual opportunities
to go in new directions.

00:23:05.977 --> 00:23:07.020
So while it seems like

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:09.560
we've been doing the
same thing all the time,

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:10.665
or for a long time,

00:23:10.665 --> 00:23:12.108
more than two decades,

00:23:12.108 --> 00:23:13.077
in fact,

00:23:13.077 --> 00:23:15.970
the kinds of questions
that have been pursued

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:18.943
have changed quite dramatically over time.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:22.280
- Right. Yeah going from, you know,

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:24.610
is there a supermassive
black hole there at all,

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:26.820
to can we test, you know,

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.570
does general relativity apply

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:32.283
near the supermassive black
hole, and so on, right?

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:37.630
- Yeah. Today we're looking at
black hole accretion physics.

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:39.680
We're looking at tidal interactions.

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:44.089
We're looking at star formation
in extreme environments.

00:23:44.089 --> 00:23:47.950
We're looking at dynamical interactions.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:51.543
I mean, among other things.

00:23:52.581 --> 00:23:57.310
And of course the new test
of GR, really exciting.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.210
And now we're on the hunt for dark matter.

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:04.101
So it's been an incredibly rich adventure.

00:24:04.101 --> 00:24:07.384
So it's been an incredibly rich adventure.

00:24:07.384 --> 00:24:08.460
- Well, it's interesting to me

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:10.773
because I think naively,

00:24:10.773 --> 00:24:13.510
you might expect that black holes

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:14.720
would be sort of a dead end

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:18.130
because they're black and
you can't see inside them,

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.180
so you don't know what's going on, right?

00:24:21.309 --> 00:24:24.040
But we've had so many
interesting observations, right?

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.130
To reveal more details of these objects

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:29.043
in all these different
inventive ways, right?

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:32.070
So I'm curious if you have any thoughts

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:35.470
about how we've been able
to make so much progress.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:37.050
And do you think that people, you know,

00:24:37.050 --> 00:24:40.750
when black holes first
appeared in physicist's minds,

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:42.310
do you think people expected

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:45.823
that we'd be able to understand
them as well as we do now?

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:50.130
- Well that's an interesting question

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.130
about what people understood.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:53.226
I mean,

00:24:53.226 --> 00:24:56.026
I think scientists are
incredibly optimistic in general.

00:25:05.186 --> 00:25:06.070
I mean, I think for a long time,

00:25:06.070 --> 00:25:07.540
there's an understanding that technology

00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:08.440
is always going to drive

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:11.453
your understanding of science further.

00:25:13.631 --> 00:25:14.681
So who knows? I mean,

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:19.120
I'm sure they didn't
imagine where we are today.

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:19.953
I mean, we couldn't,

00:25:19.953 --> 00:25:21.620
we didn't even imagine where we were today

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:23.110
when we started this experiment.

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:25.473
So, you know, half a century ago.

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:28.760
But that's one of the
wonderful things about science

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:30.410
is it evolves so quickly.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:32.470
I mean, and especially in an astrophysics.

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:33.486
I mean,

00:25:33.486 --> 00:25:36.350
if we think about how far our
understanding of the universe

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:41.220
and all the phenomena that
are in it have evolved,

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.477
it's rather remarkable.

00:25:43.477 --> 00:25:44.990
- Yeah.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:47.560
It seems to be one of those situations

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:51.180
where black holes are just
so fascinating to physicists,

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.690
that they throw everything,
every technology,

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:57.560
every tool that might be
useful at understanding them.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:01.505
And then we get some really
cool science out of that, so.

00:26:01.505 --> 00:26:05.280
Well, as you mentioned earlier, you know,

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:06.560
when you were doing your measurements

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:08.170
and when you're beginning
to do these measurements,

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:10.900
no one had ever done them before, right?

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:13.310
I'm curious if you ever felt like

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:15.470
you were on the wrong track,

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:17.520
were you worried that
you might not succeed?

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.930
What was that like for you?

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:20.900
- Oh gosh, yes.

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:22.030
I mean I wish, quite frankly,

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.484
I wish I'd kept a diary
through this whole experiment

00:26:24.484 --> 00:26:26.033
because it's been,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.950
I guess today at 27 years
of lots of highs and lows

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:34.883
and uncertainty and
delight all along the way.

00:26:35.738 --> 00:26:39.230
And there are definitely ebbs and flows

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:42.103
throughout this whole project.

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:47.079
I think there was a time

00:26:47.079 --> 00:26:51.820
right before adaptive optics came online

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:53.920
and we were still in the speckle mode

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:55.970
where that wasn't, you know,

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.300
in some sense it's like we
were getting to the limit

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:01.729
of the return for speckle imaging.

00:27:01.729 --> 00:27:03.600
And I think that was,

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:05.860
that was an interesting lull.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:10.840
We did know adaptive optics was coming.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:14.670
And, you know, we were
pushing forward on that front.

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:18.710
But you know,

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.940
it's always scary before a new technology

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:23.890
actually becomes
scientifically productive.

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:27.770
Is it actually going
to deliver the promise?

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:32.530
And I'd been working with
adaptive optics for 15 years

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:34.590
before it became
scientifically productive.

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.400
So, you know, you're never sure.

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:39.170
So I think that was one of
the really exciting things

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:41.764
about the Keck system,

00:27:41.764 --> 00:27:44.350
is that unlike a lot of earlier systems,

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:49.010
it really was well-designed
in terms of doing something

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:52.720
that was technically robust
and scientifically mature,

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.720
rather than being just another step

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:57.560
in terms of technology development,

00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.863
it became scientifically productive.

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:02.700
- You touched on this quickly earlier,

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.240
but I wanted to give you a chance

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:06.350
to go into a little more detail

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.086
about some of the new questions

00:28:09.086 --> 00:28:11.820
that your research has opened up

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:16.820
and what big puzzles kind of
await that you're looking at.

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:19.210
- Oh, there are so many.

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:22.150
I mean, I love working
with new technologies

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:24.880
because new technologies give you

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:28.050
this very different view on the universe.

00:28:28.050 --> 00:28:29.810
And often you're quite surprised

00:28:29.810 --> 00:28:33.030
when you look at something
in a new and different way.

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:37.520
So some of the first problems
that we're still pursuing

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:40.960
actually hark back to
the original questioning

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:44.023
about supermassive black
holes from the young stars.

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:46.460
So the closest,

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:48.210
some of the closest stars
that we've discovered

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:50.833
to the supermassive black
hole are actually young.

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:57.020
So this exaggerates some
of the early concerns

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:00.870
or the tension between

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:02.750
the existence of a supermassive black hole

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:05.270
and the existence of young stars.

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:07.670
So the densities that you need

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:10.540
to overcome the tidal
forces from a black hole

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:13.130
scale as your distance cubed.

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:17.510
So if you bring your young
stars that you've discovered

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:19.230
from being quite farther,

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:21.663
far away to where we are today,

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:25.330
the densities required

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:30.330
are roughly 10 to the eighth times higher

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.900
that you need to overcome the tidal forces

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:33.890
than are seen today.

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:35.800
So I call that the tidal, sorry,

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:38.113
I call that the paradox of youth.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.280
So understanding how you get young stars

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:42.620
in this extreme environment

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:44.570
is a really intriguing problem.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:48.950
And it's been super interesting

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:53.250
because people have produced
theories for explaining this

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:55.790
from all different angles.

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:56.653
It's really,

00:29:57.560 --> 00:30:00.280
it's inspired a lot of
theoretical thinking

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:04.480
and in three fundamentally different ways.

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:09.480
So one idea is that these
really are young stars,

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:12.820
but you formed them far away,

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:15.380
far away where the tidal
forces are really low.

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:20.380
So that requires you to think
about a dynamical process

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:23.580
that brings these young
stars in really quickly.

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:25.590
So you need some sort of express bus

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:27.500
in terms of physical processes

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:30.540
for migrating them in very quickly.

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:31.920
And that's actually quite difficult to do.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:34.680
And you have to invoke an
intermediate mass black hole

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:39.060
between the supermassive and
the stellar-mass black holes.

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:40.330
So they may exist,

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:45.330
but we have yet to really
have ironclad evidence.

00:30:46.070 --> 00:30:48.420
So that's a possibility,

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:50.330
although lots of questions.

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:52.300
Another idea is that maybe these things

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:53.480
that we're seeing as young stars

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:55.090
aren't young stars,

00:30:55.090 --> 00:30:59.030
they're old stars which
are masquerading as youth.

00:30:59.030 --> 00:31:00.160
And the idea there is,

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:01.630
that old stars have had lots of time

00:31:01.630 --> 00:31:04.400
to form someplace else and migrate in.

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:06.773
So that's certainly a possibility.

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.220
And then the third possibility

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:10.370
is that these are young stars

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:12.110
that really formed where you see them,

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:13.380
so in situ,

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:14.640
and that the gas densities,

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:16.040
when they were being formed,

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:17.780
was much higher, you know,

00:31:17.780 --> 00:31:20.130
by the 10 million or sorry,

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:23.440
a hundred million times
higher than we see today.

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:28.060
And as we kept making our
measurements of orbits,

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:29.900
there was an important signature

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.640
that evolved for these young stars

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:36.070
that were distributed
through this central region,

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:37.960
which is roughly half of the young stars

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:42.150
seem to reside in a disk,

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:44.420
so have orbits that all lined up.

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:49.420
So in fact, it looks a lot
like a scaled up solar system.

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:51.453
And so in fact,

00:31:51.453 --> 00:31:54.380
you can borrow some ideas
from planet formation

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:56.490
or the formation of our own solar system,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.920
where some of these
planets might've formed

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:00.720
out of an accretion disk

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.270
that existed at the time of formation.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:04.549
And in fact,

00:32:04.549 --> 00:32:05.970
this is the most popular idea today

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:09.120
for explaining the young stars
at the center of the galaxy,

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:12.470
is that our galaxy might've
had a very thick accretion disk

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:14.418
a few million years ago,

00:32:14.418 --> 00:32:19.200
which would not only have
formed these young stars,

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:23.540
but also could have
supplied a reservoir of gas

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.460
to acreete on to the central black hole,

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:27.470
making our black hole

00:32:27.470 --> 00:32:30.200
seem much more like an
active galactic nuclei

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:31.280
than it does today.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:32.710
Suggesting that the whole galaxy

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:36.660
was much more active in
its youth than it is today.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:41.660
Another mystery surrounds the old stars.

00:32:42.728 --> 00:32:46.770
The old stars, as I mentioned before,

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:49.020
should be congregated
around the black hole

00:32:49.020 --> 00:32:50.970
and should form something called a cusp,

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:52.790
an enhancement of their densities.

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.530
The local concentration of old stars

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:57.530
should be great near the
supermassive black hole.

00:32:57.530 --> 00:32:58.363
And in fact,

00:32:58.363 --> 00:33:00.120
we look for that concentration of light

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:04.345
that comes from that
enhancement of the crowding,

00:33:04.345 --> 00:33:06.023
in other galaxies.

00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:08.890
But we don't see that in the old stars.

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:10.860
We actually see a dearth of old stars

00:33:10.860 --> 00:33:12.560
at the center of the galaxy.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:14.750
And so that's been yet another mystery.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:16.470
How do you explain this phenomena

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:21.030
that we think is a prediction

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:23.730
of having a super massive black hole

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:26.723
at the center of a galaxy that we rely on.

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:30.090
And that might have to do with,

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:31.890
there's again, a couple of ways out.

00:33:34.780 --> 00:33:39.780
Either something dynamically
perturbed the galaxy

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:41.500
in the recent past.

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:45.390
So maybe we accreted a small galaxy

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:48.600
that messed up this local concentration,

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:51.840
or these old stars that we can see

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.140
were stripped of their outer atmospheres,

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:55.990
making them seem faint.

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:58.120
And then the last idea

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:01.370
is that the cusp resides in
objects that we can't see,

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:03.660
this is called the dark-cusp problem.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.885
And that's an intriguing idea

00:34:06.885 --> 00:34:10.096
that we're now trying to sort out

00:34:10.096 --> 00:34:12.380
with the measurements of stellar orbits,

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:14.020
because that dark cusp

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:19.020
should actually impact
the way the stars orbit.

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:24.070
So that is a second very intriguing idea.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:26.613
The third,

00:34:26.613 --> 00:34:28.260
the third that I'll share
and I'll stop there,

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:32.335
but there's countless other directions,

00:34:32.335 --> 00:34:37.335
is a class of objects
that were not predicted.

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:39.460
There were no predictions for these,

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:40.820
unlike the young and the old,

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:42.523
where we had clear predictions.

00:34:43.420 --> 00:34:45.890
These are objects that
when they come through

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:48.200
their closest approach with a black hole,

00:34:48.200 --> 00:34:50.060
they seem to be tidally distorted,

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:52.810
which is incredibly fun,

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:55.070
to be able to see things evolve over time.

00:34:55.070 --> 00:34:59.240
So not only do we see stars
orbiting on a human timescale,

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:03.480
but we see these stars
become tidally elongated

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:05.270
as they get near the black hole

00:35:05.270 --> 00:35:07.770
and then become compact again.

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:09.260
And this is surprising.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:11.840
And this was only with the
advances of the ability

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:15.010
to see images at different wavelengths,

00:35:15.010 --> 00:35:17.050
at longer wavelengths,

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:19.360
as well as the spectroscopy.

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:21.588
And what we think these are,

00:35:21.588 --> 00:35:24.940
are objects that merged.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:28.260
So most stars are actually
formed as binary stars.

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:30.010
If you put a binary
star near a black hole,

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.720
you get a three-body interaction

00:35:33.087 --> 00:35:34.410
and that three-body interaction

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:37.624
could cause the binaries to merge.

00:35:37.624 --> 00:35:40.140
And during that merger process,

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:42.450
what happens to the
star is it gets bloated,

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:43.960
gets really fat,

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:45.610
about a hundred times larger

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:47.190
than the other stars that we see,

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:50.820
the more typical, the
more anticipated stars.

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:54.620
And as that very bloated
star goes by the black hole,

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:57.740
it's large enough to experience
these tidal interactions

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:02.740
and have the outer shell
affected by the black hole.

00:36:03.430 --> 00:36:04.263
And that's exciting.

00:36:04.263 --> 00:36:07.170
And this one I find super intriguing

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:11.460
because it may be lighting up a process

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:13.600
that may explain one of the mysteries

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:16.670
associated with the
gravitational wave experiment.

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:19.810
So in LIGO and Virgo,

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:22.290
they're seeing objects

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:24.540
that are more massive than they expected.

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:26.330
So stellar-mass black holes

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:27.860
that are 30 times the mass of the sun,

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:29.750
rather than 10 times the mass of the sun.

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:32.590
And also occurring at a frequency

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:34.203
much higher than they expected.

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:36.830
So most stars are concentrated

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:38.370
towards the center of galaxies.

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.360
And if you think this
three-body interaction

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:42.670
is driving merger events,

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:45.070
and mergers are the underlying phenomena

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:47.910
that you see with gravitational waves,

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:50.030
you may be lighting up a process

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:52.632
that's really important for explaining

00:36:52.632 --> 00:36:55.780
the source of gravitational wave emission.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:58.440
So it's not that we're seeing
black holes merger products,

00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:00.930
but a process that could be driving

00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:02.020
stellar-mass black holes

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:05.550
that would also reside at
the center of galaxies.

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:06.510
So I'm intrigued by that

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:07.343
because it actually,

00:37:07.343 --> 00:37:11.280
it's like when you see
something that you don't expect

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.113
in one field,

00:37:12.113 --> 00:37:14.060
providing maybe a clue

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:16.970
to an unexplained answer in another area,

00:37:16.970 --> 00:37:20.230
that's like unlocking multiple secrets.

00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:25.230
So I'm intrigued by that third
problem that we see today.

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:27.970
- Yeah. That's really fascinating.

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:28.803
I love how,

00:37:28.803 --> 00:37:31.536
and this is sort of what I
mentioned at the beginning,

00:37:31.536 --> 00:37:33.390
is that these black holes,
the questions, you know,

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:37.053
connect to so many different
other areas of research.

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:40.220
And it's also just so fascinating to me

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.860
that we can use this kind of research

00:37:42.860 --> 00:37:46.160
to understand what our
galaxy was like in the past,

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:48.200
understand the history of, you know,

00:37:48.200 --> 00:37:50.480
where we are and you know,

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:52.543
what our galaxy was like before.

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:55.300
Just so many big questions.

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:57.223
It's really fun, really interesting.

00:37:58.170 --> 00:37:59.003
- It has been,

00:37:59.003 --> 00:38:02.836
it's been an amazing journey to understand

00:38:02.836 --> 00:38:05.430
it's been an amazing journey to understand

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:08.430
what we can understand at
the center of the galaxy.

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:11.280
And I think we have a
lot to be thankful for

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:14.430
in terms of the remarkable progress

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:16.220
that's been made technologically,

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:17.560
that's opened up our ability

00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:21.123
to see this really fascinating region.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:24.030
- Well, I'm curious what you think

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:28.680
our future conception
of black holes will be.

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:30.990
I mean, will we have to revise

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:32.580
what we understand about them

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:35.760
as we keep learning more, do you think?

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:36.593
- Oh, absolutely.

00:38:36.593 --> 00:38:37.640
I mean, I think as scientists,

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:41.100
we're so drawn to things
that we don't understand

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:44.810
or where our models are limited,

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:47.310
because those are the places

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:49.190
where there's the most opportunity

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:53.340
to advance our understanding of physics.

00:38:53.340 --> 00:38:57.690
So black holes definitely
represent the limitation

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.390
or the frontier of our knowledge.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:03.670
And without a doubt, we
will be making progress.

00:39:03.670 --> 00:39:05.710
There are so many new technologies

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:08.240
that are emerging at the moment,

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:09.810
not only, you know,

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:13.770
with our ability to see the
stars with adaptive optics,

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:16.200
with our ability to
see gravitational waves

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:19.120
through LIGO and Virgo,

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:21.810
as well as the Event Horizon telescope.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.770
But we have LISA to look forward,

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:28.003
the space-based
gravitational wave detector,

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:31.790
which should open up the ability

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:33.770
to study the supermassive black holes

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:35.430
with gravitational waves,

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.930
where the ground-based
gravitational wave experiments

00:39:37.930 --> 00:39:38.880
are really focused on

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:41.790
or sensitive to the
stellar-mass black holes.

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:45.120
So I think that will
be remarkably exciting.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:46.430
And then in my own area,

00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:48.290
I'm extremely excited about

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:52.270
the next generation of large
ground-based telescopes.

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:54.023
So the 30 meter telescope,

00:39:57.210 --> 00:40:00.410
as it will allow us to
see the full population.

00:40:00.410 --> 00:40:02.900
I mean, today we can only
see the brightest stars

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:04.450
at the center of the galaxy.

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:09.450
And the way I like to explain
what I think the potential is,

00:40:09.510 --> 00:40:13.180
is it's like I'm trying
to understand the economy

00:40:13.180 --> 00:40:17.600
by only being able to see
the largest transactions,

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:18.970
which are big,

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:22.324
but few and far between
compared to the smaller,

00:40:22.324 --> 00:40:24.640
but more frequent interactions.

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:26.333
So, same things with the stars.

00:40:26.333 --> 00:40:30.200
There are lots of faint stars
and very few bright stars.

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:32.720
So today we can only
see the brightest stars

00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:36.670
because they're separated
by larger distances.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:39.940
And the fainter stars
become lost in the confusion

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:42.640
of the stellar density.

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:44.230
So at higher resolution

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:48.170
that these next-generation
telescopes should afford us,

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:52.180
we'll be able to see beyond
the tip of the iceberg

00:40:52.180 --> 00:40:55.900
and see those common everyday stars.

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:56.733
And in particular,

00:40:56.733 --> 00:40:59.270
we'll be able to see stars like the sun

00:40:59.270 --> 00:41:02.920
to understand really the
full range of activity

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:05.670
that resides at the center of the galaxy.

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:07.463
- Well, that's very exciting.

00:41:07.463 --> 00:41:11.530
It seems like there's a bright
future for this kind of work.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:12.363
I'm curious,

00:41:12.363 --> 00:41:15.160
what's your advice to young people

00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:16.830
who are fascinated by black holes

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:19.243
and want to go on to study them?

00:41:20.170 --> 00:41:23.940
- Oh, my advice to young people in general

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:26.580
is find out what excites you,

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:29.794
where your passion is, and pursue it.

00:41:29.794 --> 00:41:32.590
Anything that's interesting
is going to be hard,

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:34.490
interesting and not already done is hard.

00:41:34.490 --> 00:41:38.893
So when you have passion for something,

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:41.850
it'll motivate you to overcome

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:46.180
the inevitable obstacles that
you'll face along the way.

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:48.350
- Andrea, thank you so
much for talking with me.

00:41:48.350 --> 00:41:49.953
It's been really fascinating.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:52.200
And for the viewers,

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:55.700
you can keep up with the latest
discoveries in astrophysics

00:41:55.700 --> 00:41:58.290
by visiting us at sciencenews.org

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.650
and connecting with us on social media.

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:01.803
Thanks for joining us.

00:42:02.981 --> 00:42:05.564
(upbeat music)

